course: dictionary
lesson: 3431
title: Making words stronger
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Extremely means very, very much, to a very great degree. It makes another word stronger, so extremely hot is hotter than just hot.
explain: Writers use extremely instead of saying very very. «Silicon comes from sand made extremely pure.» Pure means having nothing else mixed in, so extremely pure means it has almost nothing else mixed in at all.
explain: To check, swap in very. If the sentence still makes sense and means the same, you have found a strong word. Extremely often sits in front of an adjective such as hot, rare, dangerous or important.
fact: Extremely means very, very much, to a very great degree.
fact: Extremely is an adverb: it tells you more about an adjective, as in extremely hot.
fact: Phrases to remember: extremely important, extremely dangerous, extremely well.
fact: «An MRI scanner uses an extremely strong magnet» means the magnet is very, very strong, so metal must stay outside the room.
fact: Do not write very very. Use extremely instead.
exercise: «An MRI scanner uses an extremely strong magnet.» What does extremely mean here? | Made of metal | A little bit strong | Weak and soft | *Very, very strong
exercise: Which word means the same as extremely? | Hardly | Slightly | Never | *Very much
exercise: In «He was extremely happy when he won.», which word does extremely make stronger? | he | was | *happy | won
test: «The DNA in your cells is extremely thin and tightly coiled.» What does extremely tell us about thin? | It is not thin at all | *It is very, very thin | Thin is a little less thin | It is thick and heavy
test: Which is the best choice for a strong piece of writing? | Extremely very important | *Extremely important | Very very important | Important extremely very
exam: Which sentence uses extremely correctly? | The extremely lake was cold. | *The lake was extremely cold. | The lake extremely was cold. | The lake was very very cold.
